The default `JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter` reads the `scope` or `scp` claim and creates `SCOPE_read`, `SCOPE_write` style authorities. If your authorization server uses a `roles` claim with `["ADMIN", "USER"]`, Spring Security doesn't find any recognized authority claim and creates an empty authority set — authentication succeeds but all role checks fail.

Fix this with a `JwtAuthenticationConverter` bean:

```java
@Bean
public JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtAuthenticationConverter() {
    JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter grantedAuthoritiesConverter =
        new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();
    grantedAuthoritiesConverter.setAuthoritiesClaimName("roles"); // read from "roles" claim
    grantedAuthoritiesConverter.setAuthorityPrefix("ROLE_");       // prefix for hasRole()

    JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtAuthConverter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();
    jwtAuthConverter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(grantedAuthoritiesConverter);
    return jwtAuthConverter;
}
```

Spring Security auto-detects this bean and uses it — no additional wiring needed. After this, `hasRole("ADMIN")` works as expected because the converter creates `ROLE_ADMIN` from the `roles` claim value `ADMIN`.

For complex claim structures — authorities spread across multiple claims, nested objects, or runtime logic — implement `Converter<Jwt, Collection<GrantedAuthority>>` directly and inject it into `JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter`:

```java
grantedAuthoritiesConverter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {
    List<String> roles = jwt.getClaimAsStringList("roles");
    List<String> perms = jwt.getClaimAsStringList("permissions");
    // combine both lists into GrantedAuthority objects
});
```

The converter runs after signature validation — the JWT is already trusted. The resource server trusts claims issued by the authorization server entirely. This is why the authorization server's security is the root of trust: whatever roles it puts in the JWT, the resource server accepts.
